  "strategy": "We choose to use a quantum annealing-inspired approach that explores the sequence space more randomly but with structured learning. The core idea is to maintain a Gaussian Process (GP) surrogate model of the score function, sampled from different sequence types (e.g., equispaced, asynchronous updates, window-based), and use Bayesian optimization to find the minimal score. This approach can escape local minima better than gradient-based methods through the random exploration guided by the GP.\n\n1. Approach and rationale:\n   We use Bayesian optimization to minimize the objective function over a set of sequences. Sequences are represented as normalized vectors of fixed length (initialized from parent or common lengths). We build a GP regression model of the score function and use an acquisition function to balance between neighborhood search and length variations. We define several \"initialization strategies\" to diversify the search distribution.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Let a[\u00b7] be a normalized sequence with n terms. The score is:\n        F(a) = 2.0 * n * max(convolve(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2\n   We consider n fixed during optimization (scheduled dynamically) but allow diversification through initialization strategies.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Initialize with parent or a fixed-length sequence if parent is unavailable.\n   - Normalize the sequence.\n   - Define a set of initialization strategies for the first point.\n   - Use GPSurrogate from SciPy to optimize the sequence space:\n        model = gp_minimize(objective, sequence_initial_guess, ...)\n\n   Helper functions will be defined for:\n        objective(sequence): computes the given score.\n        initialization_strategies: use the six methods (comparable to the previous parent strategy) with fallbacks.\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Start with initialization strategies: uniform, battle piecewise, etc.\n   - Use default budget splits: GP optimization for 50% budget, but we condition on improvements.\n   - Allow length changes only if the score improves below a certain threshold.\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Allocate 100 seconds for each initialization strategy and the core optimization.\n   - Continue until either a strong improvement is found or budget exhaustion.\n\n6. Validation and return:\n   - Return the best sequence found, re-verified by the objective function.\n   - Use positive normalization (clip to [0,1000]) and handle zeros appropriately.\n\nThe entire implementation is as follows, built for readability and modularity.",
